challenge.consistency_notes:
The rules are applied to humanism as to the hard cases.

- No counted humanist text resembles any of the listed hard cases:
  - a kinsman free of bloodguilt (Numbers 35:26 to 27);
  - a zealot rewarded (Numbers 25 with Psalm 106:30 to 31);
  - a master's killing left without retaliation (Abū Dāwūd 4361);
  - newborns drowned 'by reason' (Seneca, De Ira 1.15.2, Tier 1 for Stoicism);
  - killing an icchantika or slanderers without retribution (Mahāparinirvāṇa Sūtra);
  - hostility to the follower of the Lie (Yasna 31.18, 33.2);
  - rites against enemies (Atharvaveda, Bṛhadāraṇyaka 6.4.12).
- The nearest humanist analogue to Seneca is the writing of Fletcher and Singer on disabled newborns (rows 18 and 19). These are individuals' works, outside the corpus and unverified, and no humanist body was found adopting them. They are kept out by rule 1, just as the Mahābhārata, the Pahlavi books or Musonius Rufus cannot decide for their traditions. Had a counted humanist text approved such a killing, rule 2 would make the label Contradicted, the same rule applied to De Ira.
- The same rule 1 also keeps the humanist bodies' explicit condemnations (rows 13, 15, 16, 17 and 24) from deciding in humanism's favour. The labels rest on counted texts only.
- Two symmetry points need the project lead's attention:
  1. Historic texts. Historic Tier 1 humanist texts were counted towards Taught in the households annex (L2). Supersession therefore cannot be used to set aside an unhelpful historic text such as Manifesto II's euthanasia clause. Here exclusion (d) does the work, and decision 7 forbids a later authority from outweighing a contradicting counted text. Stoicism was treated the same way: Musonius was not allowed to cancel Seneca.
  2. Branches. The 2022 Declaration is treated as speaking for the whole tradition, as in Article I, because no branch is shown to reject it. If the Council for Secular Humanism is held to be a separate major branch, it must then show its own counted text, as Digambara Jainism had to in Article I.
- On K3, humanism rests on a general clause that names neither killing nor war. Any ruling on whether such clauses reach conduct in war must apply equally to every tradition whose K3 result rests on the same kind of clause. Traditions whose counted rules name killing itself are a stronger kind of case.
